  "notes": [
    "Diocese name 'The Episcopal Diocese of Western Massachusetts' is inferred, not printed anywhere in the source's own extracted text -- confirmed from the bishop named on the first line (The Rt. Rev. Douglas J. Fisher, Western Massachusetts's own real diocesan bishop) and Josh's roster identification, same disclosed-inference situation as Atlanta and Kentucky in earlier batches. The source instead names a DIFFERENT diocese, 'The Episcopal Diocese of Massachusetts' (the separate Eastern Massachusetts diocese), as one week's own subject (February 1) -- not to be confused with this file's own diocese.",
    "Self-contained calendar-year 2026 cycle, all 52 Sundays, January 4 - December 27.",
    "Source is a three-column table (Congregations/Ministries & Partners in Ministry | Sundays | The Church & wider mission) whose linearized text extraction runs each week's three columns together with no reliable delimiter beyond the source's own punctuation; the recurring page-header text ('Congregations, Ministries, & Partners in Ministry / Sundays / The Church & wider 2026 / mission') that repeats at each page break in the raw extraction is page-layout noise, not diocese content, and is excluded entirely.",
    "Each week's first (leftmost) column item -- almost always a specific congregation, 'Name, City' -- is recorded as this corpus's own `parish` subject (place split from the trailing city name). Two exceptions: February 1 names a different diocese entirely ('The Episcopal Diocese of Massachusetts', no place) and December 13 names an urban ministry organization rather than a parish church ('Episcopal City Mission', Boston) -- the latter is still recorded as `parish` since it fills the identical structural slot as every other week and 'mission' is explicitly within this schema's own `parish` type definition (a parish, mission, or cathedral).",
    "Each week's remaining local-ministry item(s) and wider-church-and-mission item(s) are recorded as separate `category` subjects, split on the source's own semicolons (its own delimiter between distinct named organizations/offices within one column) -- an 'X and Y' pairing naming one person/couple together (e.g. 'The Rt. Rev. Douglas J. Fisher and the Rev. Betsy Fisher', January 4) is kept as ONE subject, not split, since 'and' here joins one pairing rather than separating two distinct items the way the source's own semicolons do elsewhere.",
    "'St. Paul's Gardner' (July 12) is missing the comma every other week's parish line has between congregation name and city -- read as 'St. Paul's', place 'Gardner', the same as every comma-separated week, not a different kind of entry.",
    "December 13's wider-mission column ends with a bare trailing semicolon ('Episcopal Service Corps (ESC);') and no second item after it -- read as a single subject, the trailing semicolon treated as a source-side truncation artifact, not evidence of a second, unprinted organization to guess at."
  ],
